  "prompt_text": "The figure compares a hierarchical hollow multishell structure with simpler hollow spheres and tube-like shells, and then highlights what such nested shells can do. The top part shows that a HoMS contains multiple concentric shells arranged in space and time, unlike a simple hollow particle with separate components or a tube structure. This emphasizes the extra structural hierarchy provided by several shells sharing one overall particle.\n\nThe lower sketches summarize possible functions of that architecture. One example shows catalysts placed on different shells so reactants and intermediates can be transformed stepwise in confined space, suggesting improved tandem or cascade reactions. Another shows two light absorbers distributed across inner and outer shells, with one tuned to shorter wavelengths and the other to longer wavelengths, indicating broader light harvesting. A drug-delivery schematic illustrates that molecules stored in different shell regions can be released at different rates, enabling fast and slow release from one carrier. The final example places lithium-storage materials on different shells, contrasting capacitive and battery-type behavior, which suggests combining complementary electrochemical responses within one particle. Overall, the figure conveys that multishell hollow structures offer spatially organized, multifunctional integration beyond simpler hollow geometries.",
